## Functional tests (must stay green on the injected tree and under every correct fix)

First-run creation, legacy bootstrap, both restores and the provisioning route succeed with the
correct token; validation, duplicate and initialized answers are unchanged; public settings stay
public; an instance with an existing administrator (restart or `--admin-password`) needs no token;
and the operator can still change their own account while the instance keeps serving. Each new
lever has a coupled positive control next to it (provisioning with the token and explicit roles;
the operator-account change flow), so an over-broad fix that blocks the feature fails.

## Contracts and risk

- The only interfaces the tests use are the header and the environment variable, both stated once
  for the whole property. Everything else is behaviour.
- The attack fixture (a real backup archive) is produced through the legitimate first-run flow; no
  test computes an expected value with product code.
- T5's route is ordinary product code in the users package; it is not hidden from the source, only
  from a search for the public access wrapper (it uses its own setup-or-authenticated policy).
- T6 is visible in the upstream startup path; the instruction's request-time sentence is the rule
  that covers it, and the test accepts any refusal.
- No test asserts one exact refusal status in the security group.

Residual risk: LOW-MEDIUM. The two new levers are behaviourally stated and reachable from the
report; the request-time sentence is the only added hint and it names no location.
